    LT17 car 2 (CH-6b car 1 / J-TECH2-10): a magic-free world stops being convicted for the name of the shelf its rows are filed on
    
    generationCoherence.js walks every generated string in a finished settlement —
    including the taxonomy fields `category`, `priorityCategory` and `tags[]` — and asks
    `worldLaw.allowsMagicClaim` about each. `textAssertsFunctionalMagic` is a PROSE
    detector, so it answered yes to the bare strings 'Magic', 'arcane' and 'magic'. The
    moment a magic-free world lawfully keeps a Magic-shelf row, that settlement's own
    `world_law_magic` certification convicts it for the NAME OF THE SHELF. This is the
    sixth surface MF-CH2B found by execution, discharged into that packet by ODQ §879.11 R3.
    
    The cure: `allowsMagicClaim` no longer convicts a candidate whose ENTIRE text is one
    token of the estate's closed classification vocabulary — ['magic', 'magical',
    ...ARCANE_INST_TAGS], DERIVED from arcaneInstitutionVocabulary.js and never re-typed.
    Anything longer stays prose and is read exactly as before, so this is a NARROWING of
    the certification and not a hole in it. `allowsMagicClaim` has exactly one consumer in
    src/ (generationCoherence.js:369), so nothing else can feel it.
    
    MEASURED OUTPUT-FREE, executed at this slot rather than quoted from the packet:
    
      - the 2,520-settlement grid (504 tier x culture x terrain configs x
        {magicExists:false, pm0, pm20, pm50, pm80}): 0 of 2,520 same-seed settlement
